<div class="manifesto">
  <p class="manifesto-paragraph">
    I grew up on an archipelago where the boundary between land and ocean was never fixed — where weather
    dictated connectivity, and the machines that washed ashore taught me more than any classroom.
    I learned to code by reverse-engineering firmware on salvaged industrial drones. That
    experience shaped everything: I see software not as a product but as an ecology. Systems
    that breathe, adapt, and form unexpected relationships.
  </p>
  <p class="manifesto-paragraph">
    My work moves between deep-sea acoustic data systems, generative urban infrastructure,
    AI-assisted ecological modelling, and cryptographic protocol design. I build tools for
    creative communities — distributed, open, and designed for a post-scarcity future that
    hasn't arrived yet. I believe the most interesting technology feels organic but precise:
    what happens when a mycologist learns to code.
  </p>
  <p class="manifesto-paragraph">
    I am not interested in disruption for its own sake. I am interested in emergence —
    the moment when simple rules generate irreducible complexity. That's where I live.
    <em>null/signal</em> since 2009. Still listening.
  </p>
</div>

